was a  satellite uh  launched into orbit  which was unknown by the pilot that he  was going to encounter this whisking um  satellite just uh in his eye view and  i'm sure it was uh quite a frightening  moment for him i i tell it in the very  end of the book but you know what i  loved the most was of course the mona  lisa traveled by herself  in the entire giant  jetliner strapped to the floor  well thank you thank you for that i i  think the other question is and you'll  appreciate this as an angelino  why didn't the painting make it to the  west coast  ah good question  i think of course it was probably too  risky to you know fly it for one more uh  exhibition and of course our east coast  friends think the national gallery and  the  metropolitan museum may exceed our  provincial los angeles museum in 1963  and in fact we didn't even have the los  angeles museum of art in los angeles  until  later we have another question from a  west coast person diane from san diego  asked who influenced jackie kennedy's  interest in the arts do you think  you know i think it was her mother  her mother uh took her on frequent  field trips to museums and in fact  that's how john walker got to know  jackie kennedy is when her mother  brought her to the museum of course  they were personally led through the  museum by the gallery director i mean  this isn't just an ordinary uh field  trip to the museum  and i think that her mother influenced  her  greatly and i i can honestly say from  reading  some of her private correspondence  regarding the exhibition that she  thought about her mother quite a bit and  what a triumph she would have thought it  had been  to bring the mona lisa so america could  see her  excellent  we have a question from meg from santa  fe she would like to know what role did  president kennedy play with uh in  regards to first lady jack and kennedy's  initiative to bring the painting to the  u.s  i'm sorry what role did  did president kennedy play oh  you know  it's really remarkable in all the um  uh paperwork connected with the  exhibition there are a few things where  president kennedy's own hand is there  where he's check marks or he says okay  or this or that but it really was  jackie kennedy who masterminded i mean  this is an astounding amount of evidence  actual primary  records that document her day-to-day  activities regarding the exhibition  there's no question though that  president kennedy supported this bold  idea and considered it part of his  effort to ease tensions between france  and the united states i know many of us  know from our history class the famous  force they frappe uh conflict over uh  the french nuclear arsenal something  which kennedy was vehemently opposed to  so i think he appreciated mrs kennedy's  bold idea and backed her 100 percent  let me ask you margaret we had a number  of questions about this um  do you think there was a difference in  terms of um of  people being able to see the painting  was it available to all people was there  was segregation involved  uh in terms of visiting the painting  when it was at the national gallery do  you know anything about that versus the  met  in uh john walker was insistent that it  was just  first in line first in so it was as  people arrived they were just queued up  there was no vip access although you can  imagine his phone was ringing day and  night uh for people who wanted that  personal tour the mona lisa but no this  was really an exhibition for the people  and anyone who showed up and waited in  line sadly though the lines were quite  long and i'm sure some of the school  children you know after a five or six  hour wait were quite exhausted but i  don't think anyone who actually had a  chance to see the mona lisa during the  88-day period it was in america would  ever forget it  well last question of the evening uh  margaret is going to be on the  transportation mode of uh mona lisa a  number of folks have asked why did she  travel by ship versus traveling by air  isn't that amazing  you know there was air force one  airplane travel was commonplace by 1962  1963  it was a safer  better option for the painting to fly  but the french insisted  grand manifestations  they wanted the most  opulent  arrival for the mona lisa that they  could imagine and they wanted it to come  on the ss france the great  uh 80 million dollar luxury super liner  and there are these tremendous pictures  that are in the book of the moment the  ship pulls into new york harbor and all  the boats sort of  surrounded and the big sprays of water  and basically fireworks of celebration  when the painting arrived  uh it does astound me that the french  would consider that  a safer  safer  mode of travel for such a fragile  masterpiece i mean imagine  you know painted on poplar wood  practically buckling 450 years old by  this time  um  but no the french insisted that it  traveled by ocean liner  well margaret thank you for joining us  tonight i i just want to say a special  thanks to you because your work as an  author really aligns very much with the  white house historical association not  only because we publish the second  printing of your book but also because  you're really focused on  keeping culture uh in this psyche of the  american public helping people  understand  why different things in our culture are  important and if folks want to look at  some of the other books that margaret  leslie davis has written you'll find  that a number of them are on historical  figures in the west but also your more  recent one on the gutenberg bible is uh  is also of that that same help so we  appreciate that and being a partner with  us also at the white house historical  association thank you  thank you rhett was a joy to join you
 

